# Step 1: Verify Network Connectivity

A connectivity test was performed from Kali Linux to the Windows VM using ICMP ping.

Command:

```bash
ping -c 4 192.168.16.131
```

Result:

```
4 packets transmitted, 4 received, 0% packet loss
```

### Observation

The successful ping confirmed that:

- Both virtual machines were reachable
- Network configuration was correct
- ICMP communication was working successfully

---

# Step 2: Capture Traffic Using Wireshark

Wireshark was used to capture network traffic generated during the connectivity test.

Display filter applied:

```text
arp || icmp
```

This filter displayed:

- ARP packets used for IP-to-MAC resolution
- ICMP packets generated by the ping command

---

# ARP Analysis

Before sending ICMP packets, Kali needed to discover the MAC address associated with the Windows VM IP address.

## ARP Request

Packet captured:

```
Who has 192.168.16.131? Tell 192.168.16.130
```

### Analysis

The ARP request was sent by Kali Linux:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| Sender IP | 192.168.16.130 |
| Target IP | 192.168.16.131 |
| Destination MAC | Broadcast |

The request was broadcast across the local network because Kali did not know the destination MAC address.

---

## ARP Reply

Packet captured:

```
192.168.16.131 is at 00:0c:29:0f:3d:7d
```

### Analysis

The Windows VM responded with its MAC address:

| Field | Value |
|---|---|
| IP Address | 192.168.16.131 |
| MAC Address | 00:0c:29:0f:3d:7d |

This allowed Kali Linux to communicate directly with the Windows VM at the Ethernet layer.

---

# ICMP Analysis

The ping operation generated:

| Packet Type | Count |
|---|---|
| ICMP Echo Request | 4 |
| ICMP Echo Reply | 4 |

## ICMP Flow

```
Kali Linux
192.168.16.130

        |
        | ICMP Echo Request
        ↓

Windows VM
192.168.16.131

        |
        | ICMP Echo Reply
        ↓

Kali Linux
```

### Observation

The successful exchange confirmed two-way communication between both hosts.

